WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.532
Welcome to Module 10, Reshaping Data in Postgres.

00:00:04.532 --> 00:00:09.807
Before we begin, think about what you have already learned in this course.

00:00:09.807 --> 00:00:13.387
You learned how relational tables are organized.

00:00:13.387 --> 00:00:21.592
You learned how to filter rows, group data, join tables, use subqueries, and work with text and dates.

00:00:21.592 --> 00:00:25.587
In Module 9, you focused on cleaning data.

00:00:25.587 --> 00:00:30.127
You profiled messy values, standardized them carefully, checked quality rules,

00:00:30.127 --> 00:00:34.711
and preserved uncertainty instead of guessing.

00:00:35.189 --> 00:00:37.466
Now we are ready for a new question.

00:00:37.466 --> 00:00:42.686
Once the data is clean, is it arranged in the best shape for analysis?

00:00:42.686 --> 00:00:46.681
Sometimes we want many rows and only a few columns.

00:00:46.681 --> 00:00:51.246
At other times, we want fewer rows and several category columns.

00:00:51.246 --> 00:00:55.166
The facts may be the same, but the structure is different.

00:00:55.166 --> 00:00:58.991
Throughout this module, keep one question in mind.

00:00:58.991 --> 00:01:01.296
What does one row represent?

00:01:01.296 --> 00:01:07.024
If you can answer that clearly, pivoting and un pivoting become much easier to understand.
